WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
You've learned to implement deep learning algorithms more or less from

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.880
scratch using Python and NumPy and I'm glad you did that because I wanted you

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.400
to understand what these deep learning algorithms are really doing. But you

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.400
find that as you implement more complex models such as convolutional neural

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.840
nets or recurrent neural networks or as you start to implement very large models

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.960
that is increasingly not practical, at least for most people is not practical, to

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.800
implement everything yourself from scratch. Fortunately there are now many

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.120
good deep learning software frameworks that can help you implement these models.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.520
To make an analogy, I think that hopefully you understand how to do a matrix

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.300
multiplication and you should be able to implement, you know, how to code to

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.920
multiply two matrices yourself. But as you build very large applications you

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.960
probably not want to implement your own matrix multiplication function but

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.940
instead you want to call a numerical linear algebra library that could do it

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.300
more efficiently for you. But it still helps that you understand how multiply

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.140
two matrices work. So I think deep learning has now matured to that point

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.620
where it's actually more practical you be more efficient doing some things with

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.820
some of the deep learning frameworks. So let's take a look at the frameworks out

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:17.460
there. Today there are many deep learning frameworks that makes it easy

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.660
for you to implement neural networks and here are some of the leading ones.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.060
Each of these frameworks has a dedicated user and developer community and I think

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.220
each of these frameworks is a credible choice for some subsets of applications.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.980
There are a lot of people writing articles comparing these deep learning

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.020
frameworks and how well these deep learning frameworks changes. And because

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:47.060
these frameworks are often evolving and getting better month to month, I'll leave

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.820
you to do a few internet searches yourself if you want to see the

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.460
arguments on the pros and cons of some of these frameworks. But I think many of

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.380
these frameworks are evolving and getting better very rapidly. So rather

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:05.020
than too strongly endorsing any of these frameworks I want to share with you

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.860
the criteria I would recommend you use to choose frameworks. One important

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.660
criteria is the ease of programming and that means both developing the neural

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.500
network and iterating on it as well as deploying it for production for actual

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.220
use by thousands or millions or maybe hundreds of millions of users depending

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.340
on what you're trying to do. A second important criteria is running speeds

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:35.020
especially training on large data sets some frameworks will let you run and

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.740
train your neural network more efficiently than others. And then one

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.540
criteria that people don't often talk about but I think is important is

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:49.140
whether or not the framework is truly open. And for a framework to be truly open

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.500
it needs not only to be open source but I think it needs good governance as well.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:59.340
Unfortunately in the software industry some companies have a history of open

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.100
sourcing software but maintaining single corporation control of the software and

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.660
then over some number of years as people start to use their software some

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.940
companies have a history of gradually closing off what was open source or

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.780
perhaps moving functionality into their own proprietary cloud services. So one

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:25.060
thing I pay a bit of attention to is how much you trust that the framework will

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:30.180
remain open source you know for a long time rather than just being under the

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.820
control of a single company which for whatever reason they choose to close it

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.940
off in the future even if the software is currently released under open source.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:44.740
But at least in the short term depending on your preferences of language whether

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:50.580
you prefer Python or Java or C++ or something else and depending on what

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.620
application you're working on whether it's computer vision or natural

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:59.540
language processing or online advertising or something else I think multiple of

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.700
these frameworks could be a good choice. So that's it on programming

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.180
frameworks by providing a higher level of abstraction than just a numerical

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.780
linear algebra library any of these programming frameworks can make you more

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.489
efficient as you develop machine learning applications.
